{"name":"Bear Creek","hex":"#836452","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Bear Creek","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A census-designated place in Merced County, California, United States.","A village in Outagamie County, Wisconsin, United States."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Bear_Creek"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bear Creek, Alabama","blocks":[{"text":"Bear Creek is a town in Marion County, Alabama, United States. It incorporated in 1907, although the 1910 U.S. Census records indicated 1909. At the 2020 census, the population was 1,047."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Marion_County_Alabama_Incorporated_and_Unincorporated_areas_Bear_Creek_Highlighted_0104852.svg/330px-Marion_County_Alabama_Incorporated_and_Unincorporated_areas_Bear_Creek_Highlighted_0104852.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear_Creek%2C_Alabama","wikidata":"Q66397"},{"title":"Bear Creek, Alaska","blocks":[{"text":"Bear Creek is a census-designated place (CDP) in Kenai Peninsula Borough, Alaska, United States. At the 2020 census the population was 2,129 up from 1,956 in 2010. Bear Creek is a few miles north of Seward near the stream of the same name and its source, Bear Lake."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Bear_Creek_CDP_%2832807991347%29.jpg/330px-Bear_Creek_CDP_%2832807991347%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear_Creek%2C_Alaska","wikidata":"Q2117474"},{"title":"Bear Creek (Colorado)","blocks":[{"text":"Bear Creek is a tributary of the South Platte River in central Colorado in the United States. It begins as a small creek up in the Mount Blue Sky Wilderness in Summit Lake and makes its way through Evergreen, CO, Kittredge, CO, Idledale, CO and Morrison, CO before entering Bear Creek Lake Park, managed by the City of Lakewood, Colorado."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Bear_Creek_in_Lair_o_the_Bear_Park.jpg/330px-Bear_Creek_in_Lair_o_the_Bear_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear_Creek_(Colorado)","wikidata":"Q14684299"},{"title":"Bear","blocks":[{"text":"Bears are carnivoran mammals of the family Ursidae. They are classified as caniforms within the suborder Caniformia. Although only eight species of bears are extant, they are widespread, appearing in a wide variety of habitats throughout most of the Northern Hemisphere and partially in the Southern Hemisphere. Bears are found on the continents of North America, South America, and Eurasia. Common characteristics of modern bears include large bodies with stocky legs, long snouts, small rounded ears, shaggy hair, plantigrade paws with five nonretractile claws, and short tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ours_brun_parcanimalierpyrenees_1.jpg/330px-Ours_brun_parcanimalierpyrenees_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bear","wikidata":"Q11788"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Landing_Fields_-_Alaska_-_Bear_Creek_-_NARA_-_68158700_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Landing_Fields_-_Alaska_-_Bear_Creek_-_NARA_-_68158700_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Landing_Fields_-_Alaska_-_Bear_Creek_-_NARA_-_68158700_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Landing_Fields_-_Alaska_-_Bear_Creek_-_NARA_-_68158700_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Landing_Fields_-_Alaska_-_Bear_Creek_-_NARA_-_68158700_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Landing_Fields_-_Alaska_-_Bear_Cree